A||N||9||David Gamache||Running to lean and starting to feel mean!||||11||
Z||000000||David Gamache||01-26-2001||07:54 PM||||This is the second 88 fiero 4cyc that i have run into having a lean fuel mix. The scan shows a 14.6 air/fuel instead of the 14.7 mix. There appear to be no vacumm leaks so i changed the o2 on one car and still no difference.Tried fuel injector cleaner and even cleaned out the cylinders (for possible carbon build-up that might increase cylinder compression) still no change. Then tried a different map sensor even though the readings seemed ok. Havent changed the tps but the readings look good also. even the timing gears were replaced on one of the cars and still no changes. Did a tune-up, new rapidfire plugs and G.M wires only made the HC and Nox readings worst! I read something about the temp sensor if there is sealer or teflon tape used it could mix up the temp reading and cause a problem except the guages appear to be reading correctly. I am eating up cats with this hotter exhaust and i am about burnt out myself; Any ideas?||||reg||11
Z||000001||theogre||01-26-2001||08:08 PM||||Ignore the 14.6. All 87-88 R motors do that as far as I can tell. at least all Fiero do. that isn't your problem. <P>There are 2 temp sensor you need to worry about. Neither has anything to do with the gauge.<P>ECT is in the thermostat neck. MAT is in the manifold to the right of the air cleaner. You probably should replace both of these. The MAT is probably been blasted by a backfire or is carboned up. <P>These two sensors are isolated from the shell. you can use teflon or any other sealer you want on them.<P>Read the articles in my cave for more details on the DIS motor. Several articles there should give you info you need. <A HREF="http://fierocave.shorturl.com" TARGET=_blank>The Ogre's Fiero Cave</A><P>you could also have EGR problems.<P>This isn't ment to be mean. You need to get someone to teach you how to use the scanner. Many of the readings you see on it are related to each other. It's not as simple as reading the screen. I've had mine over a year and am still learning how all the numbers relate and what they are saying. And I've been working on cars 20 years...<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 01-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||David Gamache||01-26-2001||09:12 PM||||I will check the sensors you recommended and read your section on DIS. This scanner at times is difficult to understand and i do not have much to go on except the book that came with it and the factory service manual. Hopefully i will learn a little as i go along.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||01-26-2001||11:34 PM||||Is that the AutoXray scanner?<P>If MAT is even a little crappy replace it. That thing will cause all sorts of queer errors if it's not 100%.<P>you'll want to look at the ECM stuff as well.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 01-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||David Gamache||01-27-2001||12:30 AM||||Yes, the scanner is an AutoXray.  I am having a bit of a time getting used to using it.  On the older cars, all I needed was my vacuum guage.  The EGR, as I forgot to mention, checked out okay but the MAT sensor on both cars were the original.  I think you may be right.  The problem probably does lie in the MAT sensor.  Thanks again.||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||01-27-2001||01:18 PM||||replace both of MATs. Make sure the new one has a plastic cage like the originals. There's notes about why in my cave. Borg Warner caries the correct MAT look it up as an "air charge temp" sensor if you cant't find it in the computer as a MAT. <P>The autoxray is good. It's the one I have. There's better but not for the price of the gm only scanner.<P>I had all sorts of crap going on with my 87 untill I replaced that sensor. If it's covered in crap or blasted then it can read the intake air colder than it really is. This screws up the a/f ratio.<P>you want to reset the ECM after changing the sensor.<P>Just to give you an idea of how screwy these can get..... My car had an intermitant code 45, rich mix, show up everytime I idled in cold weather. I like to replace old sensors just for safety but I replaced all of them and still had the problem. Then I found out the MAT was added to the DIS motors, replaced it and haven't seen a 45 since. <p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 01-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||David Gamache||01-27-2001||06:31 PM||||I have a GM parts supplier local, actually a few blocks from my house, so first thing monday i will order the parts and see what happens.  Like I mentioned, these sensors have never been replaced and when the timing gears went out the motors did backfire. At the very least it probably would have been good to replace these sensors some time ago anyway.||||reg||7
Z||000007||David Gamache||01-31-2001||09:55 PM||||I finally had a chance to change the mat sensor on the manifold today with a delco replacement and the pcv, but the readings are the same on the fuel mix,14.6. The o2 sensor is new along with the egr, as mentioned, and i can not locate any cracks in the exhaust manifold. Is there a possibility the fuel injector could be slightly off? I have not yey run into a lean problem from an injector. I am still at a loss to solve this one.||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||01-31-2001||11:29 PM||||As I told you. ignore the a/f ratio on the scanner. It will always say Desired A/F 14.6. The ECM is programmed very slightly lean for emmisions reasons. It is always a little richer or leaner than what ever the scanner says the AF is. Remember AF ratio is dynamic.<P>Even if exact 14.6 isn't lean enough to cause a problem like this. Chewing up catalyst is usually a rich mix problem. <P>On the other hand there is something you are missing but its hard to say what without seeing it. <P>With the car off. Apply 12V to terminal G on the ALDL. that will spin up the fuel pump. See if any fuel is leaking around/thru the injector. <P>To do that test goto radio shack or someplace and get a power cord for the lighter socket. the kind without a fuse. figure out which wire is +. plug the + wire into ALDL G and THEN plug in the cord to the lighter. MAKE ABSOLUTLY SURE THAT THE CORD IS CONNECTED ONLY TO TERMINAL G.<P>also. disconnect and plug both vac lines going to the fuel vapor can. A problem with the can can also cause rich mix.<P>did you check for an ERG problem? that will screw up emissions.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 01-31-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||David Gamache||02-01-2001||01:54 AM||||The EGR has been replaced with a new delco. Only the TPS and IAC still remain orginal.I am going to run the car up to the emisions shop and have them run an off line test on the car. Perhaps the problem is already solved and i am overkilling the problem. I have only been obsessed with the 14.7 a/f mix because that had been the reading in the past on a consistant basis. The weather and air temperatures may be causing the different mixture reading and these different readings may be normal (I really need to learn more about reading this scanner and understand the combined parameters of the readings). If the cars continue to show a high HC and Nox then I will give the injector test a shot. That terminal test you describe sounds like it may come in handy. I do wish to thank you for all your advice so far. I have definetly picked up some helpful new insights.||||reg||1
